This is Sam Tagert for SE Radio. I'm here today with Wesley Barry.

12
00:00:55,990 --> 00:01:00,709
Wesley Barry is co-founder of Anker and has worked at a variety of tech companies including Salesforce, Heroku,

13
00:01:00,709 --> 00:01:02,709
and Engineard.

14
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:04,469
as an engineer and developer advocate.

15
00:01:04,469 --> 00:01:07,030
Today we're going to talk about API design.

16
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:11,830
We've talked about various aspects of APIs in previous episodes such as 612 and 542.

17
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:15,030
We'll include links to those episodes as well as a few others in the show notes.

18
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,991
Welcome Wesley. Thanks. Thanks for having me.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,950
Let's get started by defining what is an API.

20
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:27,910
I mean I think technically application protocol interface if I remember right.

21
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,950
that sounds right I mean really it's the interface that we expose to outside users right.

22
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:37,350
so most of my experience in particular is with HTTP driven APIs.

23
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:41,350
and so usually it's somebody's making a request over the web to me.

24
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:43,190
that they want some work done in some way shape or form.

25
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:47,350
and that's how they tell me what and how I should do it.

26
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:50,069
So I think APIs actually exist at various levels.

27
00:01:50,069 --> 00:01:51,910
because I think of an API as I've got a software module.

28
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:53,991
and the API is the things that I call. Yeah.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,749
To make it do things.

30
00:01:55,749 --> 00:01:57,510
and also there's command line interfaces are.

31
00:01:57,510 --> 00:02:01,910
also a form of an API because you can script them and make them programmatic.

32
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:05,749
And as you mentioned a lot of people talk about web interfaces as APIs.

33
00:02:05,759 --> 00:02:12,630
Yep. I've heard you in a talk before mention your own acronym for APIs called assumptions probably incorrect.

34
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,229
I found that rather funny.

35
00:02:14,229 --> 00:02:16,869
Can you talk about how you came up with that and what it means?

36
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:19,751
Sure. I mean, it kind of speaks to,

37
00:02:19,751 --> 00:02:22,390
I suppose, all those different levels of APIs that you were mentioning.

38
00:02:22,390 --> 00:02:27,750
whether it's in a library, on the CLI, on the web.

39
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:33,190
that at least in my own experience, when I try to make a new interface for something.

40
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:36,631
it's the sort of no plan survives contact with the enemy kind of thing. like I'll have a plan.

41
00:02:36,631 --> 00:02:39,110
I'll put together this interface, and then I'll try to use it.

42
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:42,550
and realize that I've forgotten six or eight things or something.

43
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:45,270
that there's some edge cases that weren't considered. that basically I'll end up with something.

44
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:48,869
that the experience isn't nearly what I hoped it would be that it just doesn't pan out.

45
00:02:48,879 --> 00:02:50,390
So it's one of the reasons why more.

46
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:54,150
and more I push towards trying to do lighter weight things I guess.

47
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:56,710
trying to basically have prototypes or other things that I can work against.

48
00:02:56,710 --> 00:03:01,670
Don't spend all the time to implement the interface front to back with full tests or something.

49
00:03:01,670 --> 00:03:04,309
only to find out that I've made a huge error rate.

50
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:08,309
So doing what I can to get more hands-on experience sooner.

51
00:03:08,309 --> 00:03:09,830
because for me at least I find it really hard to just think through all of.

52
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:13,990
that in the abstract. So that's kind of what I'm talking about with assumptions probably incorrect.

53
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:19,509
If I try to just do it abstractly upfront, I tend to miss things. So I feel like really getting into the nitty-gritty of it.

54
00:03:19,509 --> 00:03:22,149
and trying it and using it in anger as people say.

55
00:03:22,159 --> 00:03:27,350
like actually using it the way you think a user would use it quickly reveals the things.

56
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:29,670
that you didn't think about or overlooked.

57
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:34,229
So if I understand correctly, you're advocating for more of an iterative or agile style of development.

58
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:37,830
Yeah, I definitely think that that makes a lot of sense in this context.

59
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:42,630
You can get a foundation of something together pretty quickly.

60
00:03:42,630 --> 00:03:47,670
but yeah, the edges always end up for me at least being pretty rough until I've had some. real experience with it.

61
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:51,910
and can get some real feedback on what is working and what isn't working. So more agile is definitely the way to go.

62
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:55,190
Do you have any specific examples that come to mind of times.

63
00:03:55,190 --> 00:03:57,509
when your assumptions were not quite correct?

64
00:03:57,509 --> 00:04:04,949
I just made a mistake related to that this morning when I was working on some API design.

65
00:04:04,949 --> 00:04:06,470
So I guess that's fresh where I was going in.

66
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:11,110
and we were working on something related to specifying domain names.

67
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:15,750
and what something can and can't do basically by which domains is able to operate on.

68
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:18,229
And I sort of quickly looked in the existing API.

69
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:19,830
and checked to see what precedents we might have.

70
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:24,150
and there was an example of an array of domain strings.

71
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:26,390
and I was like great I'll just use that.

72
00:04:26,390 --> 00:04:30,390
But it turns out that I had had a conversation with my colleague that I'd totally forgotten about.

73
00:04:30,390 --> 00:04:33,110
in which he had pointed out that that's just like one of the things.

74
00:04:33,110 --> 00:04:34,950
that we want to check against.

75
00:04:34,950 --> 00:04:38,551
And so I needed actually to have an array where there was both a type and a value. Right?

76
00:04:38,551 --> 00:04:41,830
So one of the possibilities is a DNS type with a domain name.

77
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:43,510
which is very similar to what was there already.

78
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:46,230
But just putting an array of domain names would have been too limiting.

79
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:47,749
and it would have been very difficult to change later.

80
00:04:47,749 --> 00:04:50,390
So just making that small tweak to something.

81
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:53,510
where there was a type and a value will give us a lot more flexibility in the future.

82
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:56,390
where again like I was just sort of assuming oh well I'll just follow the existing example.

83
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,270
not thinking about the broader implications in these other use cases.

84
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:03,749
that I just again didn't happen to be in mind at the time.

85
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:06,710
It can be hard to get all of the context all into your brain all at once sometimes.

86
00:05:06,710 --> 00:05:08,870
I think with interfaces.

87
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:12,550
so you end up peacemeal recalling parts that you had previously forgotten.

88
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:16,230
That leads into my next question which you kind of already answered.

89
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:21,430
but the question was I've heard you talk about one of the major problems with API designs. that were too close to the problem.

90
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:23,110
I think that's a good example right.

91
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:27,590
you're looking at the narrow thing of I just want to filter some domain names.

92
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:31,111
but you take a step back and you're like well maybe people would. also want to filter other things.

93
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:32,790
Yeah.

94
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:36,390
And in that case, like the concrete example is we aren't going to support it right away.

95
00:05:36,390 --> 00:05:39,830
but we expect in the future people might want to filter on IP addresses instead of domain names,

96
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:43,590
right? Like that's a very concrete way that that might flip.

97
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,189
But yeah, in terms of being too close to the problem,

98
00:05:45,189 --> 00:05:47,510
I think that comes up all the time, right?

99
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:52,469
Like you know the domain that your interface is for probably inside.

100
00:05:52,469 --> 00:05:54,469
and out and oftent times users don't.

101
00:05:54,469 --> 00:05:58,629
And so even if you expose it in a way that seems straightforward to you.

102
00:05:58,629 --> 00:06:02,790
that's based on the assumption that they have a bunch of knowledge that they might not have.

103
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:05,749
or that they have a bunch of experience that they might not have. So I think in a lot of cases,

104
00:06:05,749 --> 00:06:09,590
you have to think about not just what technically can do all of the things.

105
00:06:09,590 --> 00:06:12,150
but what is going to be easy to discover.

106
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:15,670
or easy to understand or easy to pick up if you've never done this before.

107
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:20,870
And sometimes maybe that means parts of the interface that are just for brand new users, right?

108
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:23,830
that maybe you don't expect experienced users will really use it that much at all.

109
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:25,909
but it provides some extra support basically for people.

110
00:06:25,909 --> 00:06:28,390
that don't have the experience yet to help get them to that point.

111
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:32,710
Maybe like a really quick upand getting started script.

112
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:35,749
What pops into my mind is wire guard. I don't know if you've used it,

113
00:06:35,749 --> 00:06:37,510
but there's like a wire guard quick up or something like that.

114
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:39,350
that you just run it and it just does the thing.

115
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:42,550
but then more advanced users might want to specify more parameters.

116
00:06:42,550 --> 00:06:44,309
Does that kind of fall into your bucket?

117
00:06:44,309 --> 00:06:48,870
Yeah, I think that's a great example where there is an easy way,

118
00:06:48,870 --> 00:06:50,870
but it's not the way for everyone necessarily.

119
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:53,990
I think at least in the past, I've sometimes gotten caught up in this.

120
00:06:53,990 --> 00:06:58,950
like try to make something that will be technically good enough for everyone.

121
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:02,309
but then because it's so flexible and perhaps so complicated,

122
00:07:02,309 --> 00:07:03,990
it ends up kind of being not that good for anyone.

123
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:08,150
It's like too confusing. Yeah, I know what I want to do, but I can't figure out how to do it with this thing.

124
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:10,150
because this thing is trying to let me do any possible thing,

125
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:12,550
and I don't want to do any possible thing.

126
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:14,469
I want to do this one narrow thing, right?

127
00:07:14,469 --> 00:07:17,110
So sometimes providing something that just does that narrow thing.

128
00:07:17,110 --> 00:07:19,029
if there's enough users that need and want.

129
00:07:19,029 --> 00:07:21,189
that can be a better experience than the something for everyone.

130
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:24,790
I find software juders never have a problem introducing more complexity.

131
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:29,510
That's for sure. Yeah.

132
00:07:29,510 --> 00:07:31,510
We talked about agile/iterative development.

133
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:34,230
What are the best ways you found to get feedback from users?

134
00:07:34,230 --> 00:07:36,230
I think it's a bit of a tangent, I guess,

135
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:39,270
but for a little while, I hope to maybe come back to this at some point.

136
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:41,749
but for a little while, I was working on a board game.

137
00:07:41,749 --> 00:07:43,510
And there's something that I learned from that context,

138
00:07:43,510 --> 00:07:46,469
from the advice that I heard from other board game designers.

139
00:07:46,469 --> 00:07:49,990
which is one of the best things you can do is what they call a blind play test.

140
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:53,909
The idea is basically you sit somebody down with this board game that you're working on.

141
00:07:53,909 --> 00:07:57,350
and give them the rule book and say, "Please try to figure it out.

142
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:01,110
I'm just going to watch what you're doing and I'm not going to answer your questions.

143
00:08:01,110 --> 00:08:04,150
because if I do, it will sort of invalidate the test.

144
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:05,909
I want to just know if you're able to figure this out.

145
00:08:05,909 --> 00:08:09,351
I think in a lot of cases with APIs like the same kind of thing. if you can manage it.

146
00:08:09,351 --> 00:08:13,189
This is similar to other UX testing things that people sometimes do.

147
00:08:13,189 --> 00:08:15,189
Here is the documentation for this interface.

148
00:08:15,189 --> 00:08:18,309
Here's the interface.

149
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:20,550
Maybe here is the task that I want you to try to accomplish.

150
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:26,550
Just speak as though you're thinking aloud and try to accomplish that. I just want to see what happens, right?

151
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:29,029
And then you can see, oh, that's interesting.

152
00:08:29,029 --> 00:08:31,749
I did this with five different people and all of them got stuck on this one thing.

153
00:08:31,749 --> 00:08:35,190
probably means that there's something that isn't quite right there, right?

154
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:37,430
Or when they were talking about how they were thinking about it,

155
00:08:37,430 --> 00:08:40,389
they all use different terminology than I would use. Like that's interesting.

156
00:08:40,389 --> 00:08:43,909
Maybe that means that the way that the documentation is written isn't quite right.

157
00:08:43,909 --> 00:08:47,910
because my user base and me don't have quite the same terminology for this thing. Or maybe I should mention both terms.

158
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:51,190
Things like that. Like there's a lot of things that become revealed.

159
00:08:51,190 --> 00:08:52,710
that other things don't seem to work as well for.

160
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:54,550
because whether you want to or not,

161
00:08:54,550 --> 00:08:58,470
if you're answering questions and really interacting with someone, you're kind of like leading the witness, right?

162
00:08:58,470 --> 00:09:01,590
So, like if you notice that it seems like they're struggling a little bit,

163
00:09:01,590 --> 00:09:03,590
you'll be likely to offer them help.

164
00:09:03,590 --> 00:09:05,590
and then that's great for in the moment.

165
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:08,070
but it turns out that if everybody else is going to struggle at that point.

166
00:09:08,070 --> 00:09:10,310
you're usually not going to be in the room to notice.

167
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:11,990
that they're having a hard time and help them through it, right?

168
00:09:11,990 --> 00:09:18,150
So, yeah, I think it can be challenging to get in that position basically and say, "I'm sorry.

169
00:09:18,150 --> 00:09:22,470
This is maybe not going to be very fun for you, but it's going to be very valuable. Like, can you please just try to struggle through it a little.

170
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:25,269
But, you know, I'm not going to leave you struggling forever.

171
00:09:25,269 --> 00:09:27,990
but I do want you to try to push through at least a little.

172
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,670
I wonder if it might be worth recording those.

173
00:09:31,670 --> 00:09:34,230
so that there's nobody else in the room, so you're not tempted to influence the witness.

174
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:37,990
I definitely think so. If you have the setup for it, that's a great approach.

175
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:45,350
Sometimes APIs end up just being a simple CRUD type access for database type stuff.

176
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:46,949
What are the potential issues with that?

177
00:09:46,949 --> 00:09:48,470
And do you have any examples?

178
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,711
Sure. There's a couple things.

179
00:09:50,711 --> 00:09:54,550
one relates to some of what we were already talking about.

180
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:57,030
So I think one of the dangers I guess with CRUD is.

181
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:01,110
that you get into that trying to have something that tries to cover every single case.

182
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:07,590
and so then some of the individual things can end up becoming incredibly complicated.

183
00:10:07,590 --> 00:10:12,470
As an example, I've seen and worked on APIs where update gets pretty overloaded.

184
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:17,990
where what update is actually doing depends a lot on which particular parameters you pass in, right?

185
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:21,829
And it's not necessarily just that behind the scenes a database record gets changed.

186
00:10:21,829 --> 00:10:23,910
but it might actually kick off other follow on operations.

187
00:10:23,910 --> 00:10:28,069
and things like that depending on what the particular values are.

188
00:10:28,069 --> 00:10:29,590
That again can get really complicated.

189
00:10:29,590 --> 00:10:32,230
and make it very difficult for both the implementers.

190
00:10:32,230 --> 00:10:36,069
and operators as well as the people that are writing stuff to use it.

191
00:10:36,069 --> 00:10:39,030
to just reason about what in the world actually happens when I call update.

192
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:41,110
That's like the dreaded it depends.

193
00:10:41,110 --> 00:10:43,590
I feel like the longer you've been in software, the more you've probably heard somebody say that.

194
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:46,711
but it's sort of what happens when you call update. Well, it depends, right?

195
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:53,350
That's the buzzword of complicated software, I guess, in a lot of cases, complexity, you know?

196
00:10:53,350 --> 00:10:56,790
So, what I have liked in a lot of cases actually is to provide CRUD.

197
00:10:56,790 --> 00:10:58,550
because you're going to need it.

198
00:10:58,550 --> 00:11:02,790
but then think about actually offering other actions or other capabilities for the resources.

199
00:11:02,790 --> 00:11:08,069
The other example is kind of like what you're talking about with WireGuard, right?

200
00:11:08,069 --> 00:11:11,910
Where you want to set up and tear down and see which WireGuard setups you have.

201
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:13,590
but sometimes you want to just very simply turn it on, right?

202
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:17,829
and not necessarily worry about all the nitty-gritty of having created one and doing whatever was necessary, right?

203
00:11:17,839 --> 00:11:20,389
There are some other things like that too.

204
00:11:20,389 --> 00:11:22,230
Or another example is like with servers, right?

205
00:11:22,230 --> 00:11:26,310
If you want to reset a server, how are you supposed to do that if it's just CRUD?

206
00:11:26,310 --> 00:11:29,110
Do you call update and change this state to restarting.

207
00:11:29,110 --> 00:11:32,069
or something like that feels a little bit awkward.

208
00:11:32,069 --> 00:11:33,670
versus an action that just is please restart the server?

209
00:11:33,670 --> 00:11:36,790
So yeah, I think those are some of the edges definitely.

210
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:40,230
of like things that are not really just updating a record but actually cause something to occur.

211
00:11:40,230 --> 00:11:47,590
and also potentially things that are more like workflow based or provide a particular use case some support.

212
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:54,710
Can you expand a little bit on separating actions from state and reading state versus doing things?

213
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:58,230
Sure. I mean, the way that I've tended to do it,

214
00:11:58,230 --> 00:11:59,750
which I feel like I've gotten in arguments with people about a lot.

215
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:03,990
so I wouldn't say it's that popular, is in a standard sort of like REST interface.

216
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:09,269
a lot of times you'll see slash resources slash an identifier.

217
00:12:09,269 --> 00:12:13,910
and then slash maybe restart or something for the server example, right?

218
00:12:13,910 --> 00:12:16,790
And what I like to do actually is to segregate those.

219
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:20,710
Like the kind of way that I like to think about it in a lot of ways is.

220
00:12:20,710 --> 00:12:22,790
it's okay to do different things.

221
00:12:22,790 --> 00:12:26,310
but ideally each of the things that you're doing you do consistently.

222
00:12:26,310 --> 00:12:28,310
and you make it clear where the separation is.

223
00:12:28,310 --> 00:12:34,710
And so what I like to do for something like that would be like slashservers/ ID slactions/reart.

224
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:40,949
And so then there's a clear sort of name space basically underneath a particular server.

225
00:12:40,949 --> 00:12:42,470
where all of the actions live together.

226
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:44,629
And it makes it easier to understand.

227
00:12:44,629 --> 00:12:47,591
and identify at a glance then that this is something that follows the action pattern. Right?

228
00:12:47,591 --> 00:12:51,110
So when you're documenting and stuff, you can basically say crowd operations work like this.

229
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:55,910
When you call update on a server, you should expect to get back a serialization of that server.

230
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:59,430
If you call read on a server, it should also be a serialization of a server.

231
00:12:59,430 --> 00:13:02,949
But actions on a server don't necessarily follow those rules, right?

232
00:13:02,949 --> 00:13:04,949
It might be you just get back a reference to a job.

233
00:13:04,949 --> 00:13:06,710
that you can check on later or something else.

234
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:10,150
not necessarily a server serialization, right?

235
00:13:10,150 --> 00:13:12,870
So again, like having that clear delineation that you know that it's separate.

236
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:15,430
The other thing that's nice is like.

237
00:13:15,430 --> 00:13:20,069
if you have a nested resources where it might be like server/ something/v volume/ something.

238
00:13:20,069 --> 00:13:23,670
by putting all of the actions together into one namespace.

239
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:27,110
it also helps to prevent polluting that subnamespace if that makes sense.

240
00:13:27,110 --> 00:13:29,269
So you don't have to worry about accidentally creating an action.

241
00:13:29,279 --> 00:13:33,671
that has a name that conflicts with some other thing that you want to now nest there. Right?

242
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:37,031
all of the actions are together nicely of like here are all the methods you can call on. this thing.

243
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:41,110
versus here are the sub resources or whatever if that makes sense.

244
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,030
That makes a lot of sense.

245
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:45,670
Another question just popped into my mind.

246
00:13:45,670 --> 00:13:47,350
You were talking about URLs.

247
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:49,190
How do you handle versioning?

248
00:13:49,190 --> 00:13:52,150
I know a lot of URLs tend to have like SLV1 or SLV2.

249
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:53,990
Is that a practice you recommend?

250
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:56,150
Is that something you caution against?

251
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:59,350
Boy, how long have you got? You know, it's one of those kinds of problems.

252
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:05,750
where I think it depends a lot on what you need or want.

253
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:09,670
So, first off, what I have done at least sometimes in the past is push for.

254
00:14:09,670 --> 00:14:11,990
that to be more in the headers.

255
00:14:11,990 --> 00:14:15,670
It feels like from a semantics of HTTP perspective, that feels like a header kind of information.

256
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:19,590
like it's a meta information about the request you're making.

257
00:14:19,590 --> 00:14:21,670
But almost everyone I've talked to does not like this idea.

258
00:14:21,670 --> 00:14:25,910
among other things like there's a lot of clients. and stuff where headers are just a bit of a pain to use.

259
00:14:25,910 --> 00:14:29,590
especially if you're needing to use them on basically every request.

260
00:14:29,590 --> 00:14:32,550
and putting in the path just is better for usability.

261
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:36,550
So more often than not, I have done it more in the path.

262
00:14:36,550 --> 00:14:39,509
I think the thing that gets really tricky is like if I had my way,

263
00:14:39,509 --> 00:14:45,110
I would prefer probably to version even different endpoints with different numbers.

264
00:14:45,110 --> 00:14:47,189
instead of having to version the entire API, right?

265
00:14:47,189 --> 00:14:50,710
But that again becomes a usability nightmare basically instantly.

266
00:14:50,710 --> 00:14:54,550
But it's really hard because otherwise it's the sort of thing of like I made a change in.

267
00:14:54,550 --> 00:14:56,470
this one endpoint that is a breaking change.

268
00:14:56,470 --> 00:15:00,470
I would like to roll this out but it's not backwards compatible.

269
00:15:00,470 --> 00:15:02,470
Do I have to roll a whole major version of this API?

270
00:15:02,470 --> 00:15:04,150
And if I do roll a whole major version of this API,

271
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:08,069
do I need to also continue to operate the old version of the API?

272
00:15:08,069 --> 00:15:10,629
If so, for how long?

273
00:15:10,629 --> 00:15:12,230
Like an example of that is that I've always found interesting,

274
00:15:12,230 --> 00:15:15,590
but I don't know that I would want to do it is Stripe.

275
00:15:15,590 --> 00:15:19,509
At least historically, I believe their version numbers were all based on basically dates.

276
00:15:19,509 --> 00:15:22,550
And so when you first set up your Stripe integration, it would lock.

277
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:24,150
so that any API requests you make,

278
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:29,750
unless you change this value, get the API as it was on that day basically.

279
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:33,750
And so they're not rolling out a new version every day or whatever. but it'll be whatever the most recent version was.

280
00:15:33,750 --> 00:15:36,150
when you start using the API is the API you lock to.

281
00:15:36,150 --> 00:15:41,030
And then they try to basically maintain that API indefinitely.

282
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:42,710
So you can at some point move up to a newer version or whatever,

283
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:45,670
but the old version continues to be maintained.

284
00:15:45,670 --> 00:15:50,390
I mean that's where it gets really hairy really is once an API is out in the world. that people are using.

285
00:15:50,399 --> 00:15:53,509
making changes to it suddenly is much much more painful right.

286
00:15:53,509 --> 00:15:55,990
like you can't go and change their code for them.

287
00:15:55,990 --> 00:15:57,590
and so you don't want to cause them pain.

288
00:15:57,590 --> 00:16:01,350
so yeah like versioning is in a lot of ways like to be avoided.

289
00:16:01,350 --> 00:16:03,350
basically I think like as much as possible.

290
00:16:03,350 --> 00:16:06,870
but obviously you can't do that entirely. what I've seen in a lot of cases I think.

291
00:16:06,870 --> 00:16:11,030
that works okay is mostly focusing on major versions.

292
00:16:11,030 --> 00:16:13,430
trying to only do non-breaking changes.

293
00:16:13,430 --> 00:16:18,389
and then having a very long deprecation period if you do move to a new version.

294
00:16:18,389 --> 00:16:21,269
and then doing probably like brown outs basically where you temporarily turn the API off.

295
00:16:21,269 --> 00:16:24,710
so anybody that's still using it that didn't see the announcements will get errors.

296
00:16:24,710 --> 00:16:28,630
and have a chance to recover and respond to it. before the API is totally gone.

297
00:16:28,630 --> 00:16:33,910
but again it's pretty ownorous so mostly I try to avoid it if I can.

298
00:16:33,910 --> 00:16:36,230
which you can't really but as much as you can you try to avoid it.

299
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:40,470
it occurs to me that the biggest pain point might be changes on the back end.

300
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:42,069
that require you.

301
00:16:42,069 --> 00:16:46,550
like you change the database so these two things are no longer associated. how old API endpoints are trying to make that association.

302
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:48,471
It's no longer there. Is that something?

303
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,230
That kind of stuff can be a huge pain.

304
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:52,389
Like if you are in a situation.

305
00:16:52,389 --> 00:16:56,390
where the back end has changed drastically and the API just doesn't quite match. Yeah.

306
00:16:56,390 --> 00:17:00,629
You end up basically having to maintain all kinds of things.

307
00:17:00,629 --> 00:17:02,150
that have just logic to convert from one version to another.

308
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:07,750
Right. So I think in some cases I've kind of seen it's like here is the function.

309
00:17:07,750 --> 00:17:09,590
that takes an API call from version one.

310
00:17:09,590 --> 00:17:12,549
and translates it to version two. and here's the call that translates it from version two to version three.

311
00:17:12,559 --> 00:17:15,110
and you kind of end up in this thing. where it just sort of takes the request.

312
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:18,390
and chains it up through as many layers as it needs to.

313
00:17:18,390 --> 00:17:21,510
to try to translate it into something that would be understood in the modern context.

314
00:17:21,510 --> 00:17:25,590
But of course maintaining the testing and infrastructure to actually operate all of those things is non-trivial.

315
00:17:25,590 --> 00:17:27,270
It's a real headache.

316
00:17:27,270 --> 00:17:29,110
I mean it's you could think about it,

317
00:17:29,110 --> 00:17:32,950
you know, with other software that you work on or whatever, right?

318
00:17:32,950 --> 00:17:36,150
It's like what if you had to maintain not only whatever the latest version was,

319
00:17:36,150 --> 00:17:40,549
but like every version of that you've ever written, right? It's a real headache. So you have to like make some trade-offs somewhere.

320
00:17:40,549 --> 00:17:44,470
like you can't really actually maintain every version.

321
00:17:44,470 --> 00:17:46,789
So, which ones do you choose to maintain or not?

322
00:17:46,789 --> 00:17:49,590
And how long do you continue to maintain the old ones before you just pull the plug.

323
00:17:49,590 --> 00:17:53,830
and say we did what we could do, but if you are still aren't up to date, sorry. Like, it's just time to move on.

324
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:59,590
Another question that popped into my mind as we're talking about upgrading APIs and changing them.

325
00:17:59,590 --> 00:18:01,430
Have you used the strangler fig pattern?

326
00:18:01,430 --> 00:18:03,270
Does that sound familiar to you?

327
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:06,150
I have heard of it.

328
00:18:06,150 --> 00:18:09,110
I don't know if I've used it. I didn't call it that at the time, I don't think.

329
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:11,669
I mean, it's kind of the idea.

330
00:18:11,669 --> 00:18:14,549
that you remove more and more of the thing until there's almost none left, right?

331
00:18:14,549 --> 00:18:17,510
You kind of replace it with something else. Is that accurate or how would you describe it?

332
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:22,230
So, I would describe it as you've got your old back end. and you've got a new back end.

333
00:18:22,230 --> 00:18:25,590
and you kind of run them in parallel and compare the results. and then ship the new one.

334
00:18:25,590 --> 00:18:28,549
and then eventually after they're the same for a significant amount of time,

335
00:18:28,549 --> 00:18:30,230
then you remove the old one kind of thing.

336
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:31,910
Ah, yeah. Yeah, that makes total sense.

337
00:18:31,919 --> 00:18:35,990
I don't have that much experience doing that in particular.

338
00:18:35,990 --> 00:18:41,990
but I've seen that and I would definitely be interested in trying. that approach if I was working on a problem like that.

339
00:18:41,990 --> 00:18:44,950
I just don't happen to have a problem like that that I've worked on recently at least.

340
00:18:44,950 --> 00:18:50,071
So, I've heard you use the term API connoisseur. What does that mean to you?

341
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:52,230
Sure.

342
00:18:52,230 --> 00:18:57,510
I mean, I think it's kind of like how sometimes I just drink a cup of coffee to. drink a cup of coffee. but I'd say in general that's an area.

343
00:18:57,510 --> 00:19:00,390
where I feel like I'm a bit of a connoisseur at least.

344
00:19:00,390 --> 00:19:03,750
Like I know some things about different regions that coffees are roasted in.

345
00:19:03,750 --> 00:19:06,950
and some things about different ways that they might be fermented.

346
00:19:06,950 --> 00:19:10,789
or roasted and I have some opinions about that. So when I have my cup of coffee or whatever,

347
00:19:10,789 --> 00:19:14,070
I'm thinking about like what is it that I like or dislike about this particular brew?

348
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:16,710
Would I want to do this again? Would I want to change something?

349
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:19,190
And so in the same way with APIs,

350
00:19:19,190 --> 00:19:20,710
I think I especially early on when I was working on them.

351
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:30,549
often would just be like, well, I have been assigned the task at my work of integrating with. this thing. so I'm just going to do it, right? I'm just going to figure out what needs to happen.

352
00:19:30,549 --> 00:19:34,150
I'm going to do it and I'm going to move on, right?

353
00:19:34,150 --> 00:19:37,750
And later I eventually because I found it interesting got to more of a point of okay.

354
00:19:37,750 --> 00:19:40,230
like yes, I'm going to do that.

355
00:19:40,230 --> 00:19:42,549
But at the same time, I can say what of this API did I really like?

356
00:19:42,549 --> 00:19:44,870
What was interesting?

357
00:19:44,870 --> 00:19:46,630
what was a pain in the butt in the end when I started doing it.

358
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:51,590
like what are the parts that I like. and dislike about this why do I like and dislike those.

359
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:56,230
and I think it's very helpful then to start to develop a sort of taste for it right.

360
00:19:56,230 --> 00:20:00,789
so it's not just oh right well it's an API it is what it is. I'm going to work with it and I'm going to move on right.

361
00:20:00,789 --> 00:20:03,110
I think especially there's this challenge in.

362
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:10,150
that if you're designing APIs you don't get that many opportunities to actually do it basically.

363
00:20:10,150 --> 00:20:13,991
like especially with web APIs I mean with other APIs you get a few more bites at the. apple so to speak.

364
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,350
But like I think most people if you're designing web APIs,

365
00:20:17,350 --> 00:20:22,310
how many web APIs are you going to make in your whole career? Maybe six or eight or something at most.

366
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:24,310
Probably for a lot of people it's more like two or three.

367
00:20:24,310 --> 00:20:27,110
And so you're not going to have as many chances to make the mistakes.

368
00:20:27,110 --> 00:20:30,549
and realize that you could or should have done it differently and try again.

369
00:20:30,549 --> 00:20:34,230
And so rather than doing that in the really costly way of just the APIs you produce.

370
00:20:34,230 --> 00:20:37,510
I think there's a lot of value in trying to do what you can to be critical.

371
00:20:37,510 --> 00:20:41,190
and like learn from the APIs that are already out there to say what's an API you really.

372
00:20:41,200 --> 00:20:43,191
like? Okay.

373
00:20:43,191 --> 00:20:45,350
why what are the things that you really like about it.

374
00:20:45,350 --> 00:20:48,390
and help to get that to inform what you do in your own designs.

375
00:20:48,400 --> 00:20:53,270
So, as a connoisseur, do you have an example of a well done API that you really like?

376
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:57,750
Yeah. I mean, so in terms of my more recent work,

377
00:20:57,750 --> 00:21:00,630
a lot of what I've been working on has been CLI stuff.

378
00:21:00,630 --> 00:21:02,630
And so, an example of a CLI.

379
00:21:02,630 --> 00:21:04,310
that I often will look to just to see how they did it.

380
00:21:04,310 --> 00:21:10,070
and to get inspiration is the GH CLI, which is GitHub's CLI.

381
00:21:10,070 --> 00:21:13,029
And I mean, it's like anything when you're being critical of whatever.

382
00:21:13,029 --> 00:21:16,870
it's like I wouldn't do everything exactly the way that they did,

383
00:21:16,870 --> 00:21:19,830
but I mostly quite like what they did.

384
00:21:19,830 --> 00:21:21,350
And so, again, if I'm like, I think I want to maybe take from.

385
00:21:21,350 --> 00:21:24,549
that example and do something similar to what they did.

386
00:21:24,549 --> 00:21:27,990
I'll run through that command six or eight times and with different parameters.

387
00:21:27,990 --> 00:21:30,711
and try to really think about what is it that I actually like about this? What is the thing that I like?

388
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:33,750
Because I'm not implementing the exact same operation on my side, right?

389
00:21:33,750 --> 00:21:36,789
I'm just doing something that is maybe inspired by that, right?

390
00:21:36,789 --> 00:21:40,310
So getting a feel for it and then trying to bring that back.

391
00:21:40,310 --> 00:21:42,070
and have something that also has some of the same I guess feel.

392
00:21:42,070 --> 00:21:45,190
and same taste so to speak has been really productive.

393
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:48,390
Along those lines, is there a particular API you dread using?

394
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:54,710
I mean the most obvious example probably also from my recent work would be the OpenSSL CLI.

395
00:21:54,710 --> 00:21:56,311
Like what are the arguments? I don't even know.

396
00:21:56,311 --> 00:21:58,950
I have to look them up every time.

397
00:21:58,950 --> 00:22:01,909
and then still have not the highest confidence that I have done the right thing.

398
00:22:01,909 --> 00:22:03,990
They're just very confusing, very cryptic.

399
00:22:03,990 --> 00:22:07,830
I guess you might say the one I find most frustrating I think is Git.

400
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:10,789
Git is pretty rough. I'm not going to lie.

401
00:22:10,789 --> 00:22:15,110
I mean that's come up before when I've talked to people of like I like what Git does.

402
00:22:15,110 --> 00:22:19,909
I don't necessarily like the interface that it provides to me to actually do it. For the core stuff it's fine.

403
00:22:19,909 --> 00:22:21,430
I've done it so many times that it's muscle memory almost.

404
00:22:21,430 --> 00:22:26,070
But for anything beyond that again it's like back to documentation. and then I'm not sure that I did it correctly.

405
00:22:26,070 --> 00:22:29,669
I don't know how many rebases I've ended up messing up.

406
00:22:29,669 --> 00:22:31,510
because yours and theirs don't mean what I think that they do.

407
00:22:31,510 --> 00:22:37,830
and the order of things really matters and it's not super userfriendly despite being very powerful.

408
00:22:37,830 --> 00:22:39,750
So, the two things I dislike about it in particular,

409
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:43,750
and you mentioned one of them, is the naming, the ours and theirs.

410
00:22:43,750 --> 00:22:49,910
but also reset, revert, and restore all kind of like set things back to a previous state, but in different ways.

411
00:22:49,910 --> 00:22:54,870
and it's very, it's nice that they're all the same cuz you remember they all set something back.

412
00:22:54,870 --> 00:22:57,029
but trying to remember which one sets what back is really hard.

413
00:22:57,029 --> 00:23:02,549
And the inconsistency as well, cuz I keep remembering that for branches and tags, you delete them.

414
00:23:02,549 --> 00:23:04,950
but for sure, remotes, you remove them.

415
00:23:04,950 --> 00:23:09,669
and like that I'm always like wait which one am I doing here? So those little polish things.

416
00:23:09,679 --> 00:23:15,270
Yeah. And it's something that I think about a lot when I'm working on interfaces of just like. for me at least when I see something like.

417
00:23:15,270 --> 00:23:18,870
that where it's in this one case it says remove and this other case it says destroy.

418
00:23:18,870 --> 00:23:22,470
then I'm left thinking is there meaningful difference between these two?

419
00:23:22,470 --> 00:23:25,190
Is it just coincidence that they happen to have a different name.

420
00:23:25,190 --> 00:23:28,630
or does remove really indicate something different than delete?

421
00:23:28,630 --> 00:23:30,710
You know maybe remove means that it's not sort of connected anymore.

422
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:35,510
There's not an association anymore but the object still exists. Or maybe it actually just deletes the object.

423
00:23:35,510 --> 00:23:38,070
and they called it two different things for no apparent reason.

424
00:23:38,070 --> 00:23:42,630
It's like it makes me question how much I actually understand the interface in the first place, right?

425
00:23:42,630 --> 00:23:45,430
Which is not what you want your users to be doing, right? Do I even know what I'm doing here?

426
00:23:45,430 --> 00:23:49,029
Like I thought I did, but now you're making me question everything. That's the worst case really.

427
00:23:49,039 --> 00:23:54,310
I always go back to everything's done for a reason. It doesn't always necessarily make sense. It's not always a great reason.

428
00:23:54,310 --> 00:23:57,430
But when you don't know the reason, you're like left questioning, well, what was the reason?

429
00:23:57,440 --> 00:23:59,831
Yeah. Yeah.

430
00:23:59,831 --> 00:24:03,029
And can I ever understand what that reason was? Do I need to understand what that reason was?

431
00:24:03,029 --> 00:24:04,711
You know? Yeah.

432
00:24:04,711 --> 00:24:06,630
Is it important enough to waste time trying to figure it out?

433
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:11,510
Exactly. Speaking of surprises, what is the principle of least surprise?

434
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:16,470
I think it comes from Matts, the creator of Ruby.

435
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:17,990
So, a lot of my background is in Ruby programming.

436
00:24:17,990 --> 00:24:21,750
and it's something that I've seen talked about in the context of Matt.

437
00:24:21,750 --> 00:24:26,470
So, basically like ideally your interface is set up in such a way that.

438
00:24:26,470 --> 00:24:28,149
if you basically learn one part of it and you move to another part,

439
00:24:28,159 --> 00:24:31,830
you should be able to guess basically how things work.

440
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:37,590
So talking about our git example, you know, like delete versus remove, that violates, I guess, the principle of least surprise.

441
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:43,351
Ideally, like if you learn that getting rid of an object is done by calling delete. and you move to a new object.

442
00:24:43,351 --> 00:24:47,751
the principle of least surprised should say basically you should be able to call delete. and it should work, right?

443
00:24:47,751 --> 00:24:51,831
Or perhaps give you a meaningful message or instruct you as to how you should do it otherwise, right?

444
00:24:51,831 --> 00:24:55,669
Like you shouldn't be surprised of like wait a second like why is this called remove, right?

445
00:24:55,669 --> 00:24:59,029
Like that's just again not a good experience for the user.

446
00:24:59,029 --> 00:25:01,110
So trying to just minimize that as much as possible,

447
00:25:01,110 --> 00:25:03,669
make as much transfer between things as you can.

448
00:25:03,679 --> 00:25:07,430
To your comment about giving hints, I will give get credit.

449
00:25:07,430 --> 00:25:09,269
It's gotten better over the years at giving hints.

450
00:25:09,269 --> 00:25:12,149
Like when you're in the middle of a rebase or something,

451
00:25:12,149 --> 00:25:13,830
you can do the get status and it gives you little hints on what to do.

452
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:16,310
They're still kind of cryptic, but at least it's something.

453
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:20,789
I mean, that's been some of my worst experiences with various tools, I think, is.

454
00:25:20,789 --> 00:25:22,550
you know, the sort of you've done something wrong. Please try harder.

455
00:25:22,559 --> 00:25:25,350
Please try again. Right? It's like, well, what what do you mean?

456
00:25:25,350 --> 00:25:28,710
I I don't understand what I did wrong in the first place. What am I how am I supposed to deal with this?

457
00:25:28,710 --> 00:25:33,110
You know, my favorite programming language, Lab View, has a you get file not found error,

458
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:36,230
but it never tells you what file it can't find.

459
00:25:36,230 --> 00:25:41,590
which is always interesting because you're like, uh, you know, you're looking for it, you can't find it. Why can't you tell me so I can go find it?

460
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,831
Yep. The dreaded something went wrong.

461
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:47,350
You're like, great, thanks for letting me know, I guess.

462
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:57,029
So, talking about principal lease surprise, there's this idea of interrogation versus implicit discoverability. that seems kind of related. Can you talk about that a little?

463
00:25:57,039 --> 00:25:59,669
Sure.

464
00:25:59,669 --> 00:26:02,950
I mean, the idea with discoverability, I think, is more to that sort of like I guessed, right?

465
00:26:02,950 --> 00:26:05,990
I wasn't sure what this was going to be, so I tried a thing and it worked out.

466
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,789
versus more like I called a method.

467
00:26:08,789 --> 00:26:13,750
and it listed to me all of the options and then I chose one of those options, right?

468
00:26:13,750 --> 00:26:16,630
So, it reveals to you the possibility. I don't know. I mean, this is one of the areas.

469
00:26:16,630 --> 00:26:21,750
where I guess in some ways like I would rather not choose probably.

470
00:26:21,750 --> 00:26:23,669
It seems like a kind of improv theater both and situation, right?

471
00:26:23,669 --> 00:26:26,549
Like I think there are times where it's very helpful to be able to say,

472
00:26:26,549 --> 00:26:30,070
"Can you just tell me which operations are available here?

473
00:26:30,070 --> 00:26:32,549
but ideally you could also just guess and be fine."

474
00:26:32,549 --> 00:26:34,149
Different users resonate more with one or the other.

475
00:26:34,149 --> 00:26:37,110
You know, some users maybe would never feel like guessing.

476
00:26:37,110 --> 00:26:40,710
Maybe that's just not their thing, right? And sometimes you would never guess the thing.

477
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:43,669
because it's an operation that only exists on that one object,

478
00:26:43,669 --> 00:26:45,269
so you never would have seen it before, right?

479
00:26:45,269 --> 00:26:46,789
So yeah, I think there's a use case for both.

480
00:26:46,799 --> 00:26:50,390
Do you have any specific tips for documenting or describing APIs?

481
00:26:50,390 --> 00:26:53,990
And as I'm thinking about this, is it better to have like separate documents for that.

482
00:26:53,990 --> 00:26:59,350
or would you rather interrogate the API to figure out what's there. or do we need both?

483
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:01,510
I've done different things over time.

484
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:06,710
These days I lean towards using open API to write a spec basically.

485
00:27:06,710 --> 00:27:09,190
that describes what the API should be doing.

486
00:27:09,190 --> 00:27:12,549
And then from there, you know, there's a lot of great tooling.

487
00:27:12,549 --> 00:27:16,789
that will generate nice HTML documentation from that that you can give out to users.

488
00:27:16,789 --> 00:27:19,110
And then we even use a lot of tooling to take.

489
00:27:19,110 --> 00:27:23,750
that same spec to be able to run our test against it.

490
00:27:23,750 --> 00:27:25,269
So we actually have in testing basically like.

491
00:27:25,269 --> 00:27:29,750
if you make an API call that does not somehow match with the spec. that will just raise an error automatically.

492
00:27:29,750 --> 00:27:34,149
so that we can make sure that basically like what we're documenting lines up with what we're testing.

493
00:27:34,149 --> 00:27:35,990
which lines up with what we're actually presenting.

494
00:27:35,990 --> 00:27:39,110
And that has been really helpful to have that be something that's basically machine readable.

495
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:43,750
because then you can do interesting things with it as opposed to literally just a markdown file somewhere.

496
00:27:43,750 --> 00:27:45,909
that says what it does, right?

497
00:27:45,909 --> 00:27:49,029
which is much more prone to drifting away from what is in actuality happening.

498
00:27:49,039 --> 00:27:52,070
Yeah, stale docs definitely cause a lot of problems.

499
00:27:52,080 --> 00:27:56,789
Yes, for sure. Going back to our principle of least surprise a little bit.

500
00:27:56,789 --> 00:28:01,190
how do you balance being new and innovative and doing things in a different way.

501
00:28:01,190 --> 00:28:05,110
and sticking to what people are used to and what they expect? Give an example of that.

502
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:07,110
Sure.

503
00:28:07,110 --> 00:28:09,029
I mean, I wouldn't say that I've always gotten that balance correct.

504
00:28:09,029 --> 00:28:13,510
I mean, I think some of it goes to what the needs of the user are.

505
00:28:13,510 --> 00:28:16,230
Like I think doing anything innovative, you know,

506
00:28:16,230 --> 00:28:20,310
is highly unlikely to be something that will fit into the principle of least surprise, right?

507
00:28:20,310 --> 00:28:23,430
Like if they've never seen it before, they're not going to guess it.

508
00:28:23,430 --> 00:28:25,751
It's just not how that works, right? So there's a couple parts to it.

509
00:28:25,760 --> 00:28:31,029
One is obviously like if you are going to do something new, at least do it consistently, right?

510
00:28:31,029 --> 00:28:33,190
Don't just do something new in one place.

511
00:28:33,190 --> 00:28:38,950
and do something else that is new to do the same kind of thing somewhere else, right?

512
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:40,549
Like try to have a pattern basically.

513
00:28:40,549 --> 00:28:43,750
So that's one of the things that I guess I lean towards is like try to have a.

514
00:28:43,750 --> 00:28:45,590
new pattern, not a new one-off, right?

515
00:28:45,590 --> 00:28:50,310
So like what we were talking about with like having the actions be nested and grouped together, right?

516
00:28:50,310 --> 00:28:51,830
That's a pattern that you can repeat, you know?

517
00:28:51,830 --> 00:28:54,789
So you could have anytime there are actions anywhere in the API, they appear like this, right?

518
00:28:54,799 --> 00:28:59,669
And here's the set of rules that apply to actions so you can understand what to expect there.

519
00:28:59,669 --> 00:29:03,030
But yeah, I think some of it just comes down to like how much pain is this causing. or not?

520
00:29:03,030 --> 00:29:04,630
How much confusion is it causing or not?

521
00:29:04,630 --> 00:29:08,230
Is it worth trying to do something a little bit different to ease that?

522
00:29:08,230 --> 00:29:10,149
And also there's a lot of cases.

523
00:29:10,149 --> 00:29:14,230
where more and more I lean towards doing the again both and thing.

524
00:29:14,230 --> 00:29:15,990
right, of like maybe I can provide the old way and the new way.

525
00:29:15,990 --> 00:29:19,430
And so then I can make an argument for why the new way is better.

526
00:29:19,430 --> 00:29:24,630
And if people are actually interested and buy that argument and have those needs, they'll probably use the new way. And if they don't, they can still use the old way.

527
00:29:24,640 --> 00:29:26,389
Like it's not, you know, all or nothing.

528
00:29:26,399 --> 00:29:31,909
Like you have to get ramped up to my weird thing that might or might not be good.

529
00:29:31,909 --> 00:29:33,909
and let's, you know, in order to even use my API.

530
00:29:33,909 --> 00:29:39,990
Would an example of that maybe be like running a GraphQL server. and a regular CRUD server side by side or something like that?

531
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:44,870
Yeah, quite possibly. And I mean sometimes that's just not reasonable or realistic, right?

532
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:47,669
Because the operational cost of running more than one thing is high.

533
00:29:47,669 --> 00:29:55,669
Some of the assumptions that CRUD versus GraphQL make incompatible in terms of how you're even storing the. data, right? Like things like that.

534
00:29:55,669 --> 00:29:57,510
But yeah, I think that that could be a reasonable example.

535
00:29:57,520 --> 00:29:59,430
You mentioned being a Ruby developer.

536
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:02,310
You have a Ruby gem called Fog.

537
00:30:02,310 --> 00:30:04,470
Do you want to talk about that a little bit and what it does?

538
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:08,070
Sure.

539
00:30:08,070 --> 00:30:10,790
This is kind of the from the early days of cloud. I was a Ruby developer.

540
00:30:10,790 --> 00:30:16,230
I was working at cloud infrastructure startups.

541
00:30:16,230 --> 00:30:19,110
and I was finding that you know in.

542
00:30:19,110 --> 00:30:24,470
when AWS even was quite new and there were some other competitors just coming online.

543
00:30:24,470 --> 00:30:28,389
that the clients available to me to interact with those were left a lot to be desired.

544
00:30:28,389 --> 00:30:30,389
I guess they either were non-existent or just not very good yet.

545
00:30:30,399 --> 00:30:37,590
And so I initially set out just to add clients for a couple of the really new AWS.

546
00:30:37,590 --> 00:30:39,510
services that I didn't see any clients for yet.

547
00:30:39,510 --> 00:30:44,149
I think I want to say maybe Simple DB or something was the first one that I did.

548
00:30:44,149 --> 00:30:46,870
which I've not even heard anybody talk about in a really long time, I don't think.

549
00:30:46,870 --> 00:30:49,750
But at the time I was just like this is interesting.

550
00:30:49,750 --> 00:30:53,750
And so yeah, I kind of like dived into writing these clients just because cloud seemed exciting.

551
00:30:53,750 --> 00:30:56,630
and I wanted to learn more and I wanted to be able to use it.

552
00:30:56,630 --> 00:30:59,830
And again, I just the tools weren't there. So, at first it was just like me dabbling with.

553
00:30:59,830 --> 00:31:04,631
that and then over time it grew to be a lot of clients for a lot of different. APIs.

554
00:31:04,631 --> 00:31:10,070
As my interest continued to grow where people asked me if I could add support for something else.

555
00:31:10,070 --> 00:31:14,310
and then eventually I worked to start building abstractions on top of that.

556
00:31:14,310 --> 00:31:18,470
so that you could have some degree of interoperability sort of like the adapter pattern basically.

557
00:31:18,470 --> 00:31:20,711
of like saying I would like a server. Okay.

558
00:31:20,711 --> 00:31:24,230
well like which cloud provider would you like a server on.

559
00:31:24,230 --> 00:31:29,029
and have it be able to do at least many of the things to make it interoperable between. those providers right.

560
00:31:29,029 --> 00:31:32,711
so it would understand that when you say you want a server on Rackspace it's this API call. that you need to make.

561
00:31:32,720 --> 00:31:35,909
but if you want a server on AWS it's this other different API call.

562
00:31:35,909 --> 00:31:37,509
that you might need to make.

563
00:31:37,509 --> 00:31:41,509
that is definitely something that I've struggled with a lot is having things.

564
00:31:41,509 --> 00:31:43,509
that objects that do similar things but have different APIs.

565
00:31:43,519 --> 00:31:46,870
and trying to come up with good abstractions around.

566
00:31:46,870 --> 00:31:51,111
that what lessons have you learned from fog that might help other people with. Sure.

567
00:31:51,111 --> 00:31:55,750
It depends a lot on the nature of the objects, I guess.

568
00:31:55,750 --> 00:31:58,870
And I think this kind of relates maybe also to the action pattern I was talking about actually.

569
00:31:58,870 --> 00:32:02,950
like was which is something I kind of like stumbled upon much later.

570
00:32:02,950 --> 00:32:06,710
But the closer it is to basically just CRUD, the easier it is going to be.

571
00:32:06,710 --> 00:32:10,950
to have something that is more interoperable like a repeatable pattern.

572
00:32:10,950 --> 00:32:13,190
So a good example of that would be like cloud storage, right?

573
00:32:13,190 --> 00:32:18,549
Whether it's S3 or the many other S3 clones and competitors that now exist, right?

574
00:32:18,549 --> 00:32:22,630
like the interface to most of them is pretty comparable, you know?

575
00:32:22,630 --> 00:32:24,389
like you create folders to contain files,

576
00:32:24,389 --> 00:32:27,750
you create files within those folders, you fetch them, you update them, you know?

577
00:32:27,750 --> 00:32:30,470
like that's kind of it, right?

578
00:32:30,470 --> 00:32:34,149
So creating a more uniform interface to all of those is at least relatively straightforward.

579
00:32:34,159 --> 00:32:37,909
The thing where much trickier is like the example I gave of like servers.

580
00:32:37,909 --> 00:32:40,630
When people say server, they don't always even seem to mean the same thing, right?

581
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:43,430
Like there's a lot of different things that could mean.

582
00:32:43,430 --> 00:32:45,670
in terms of like is it bare metal? Is it virtualized?

583
00:32:45,670 --> 00:32:47,269
Is it somewhere in between?

584
00:32:47,269 --> 00:32:49,990
Does it have volumes that are attached that you need to do something with?

585
00:32:49,990 --> 00:32:52,549
Do you like put an image onto it?

586
00:32:52,549 --> 00:32:55,269
Do you put a a Docker thing onto it? Like, you know, there's a lot of different things it could mean.

587
00:32:55,279 --> 00:32:59,590
So, the challenge there definitely be became like it felt like the interface.

588
00:32:59,600 --> 00:33:03,029
that resulted was sort of this lowest common denominator thing.

589
00:33:03,029 --> 00:33:08,070
where I mean technically it can boot a server, but is it like actually a meaningful useful server.

590
00:33:08,070 --> 00:33:10,871
if you use the generic interface or do you have to get into all these particulars for. that particular service.

591
00:33:10,880 --> 00:33:14,070
in order to actually get it to do what you want?

592
00:33:14,070 --> 00:33:16,711
At which point, how much value is this really adding? So, I don't know.

593
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:22,871
Like, it was definitely an interesting thing to explore and there are parts of it that work great. but there's other parts that. Yeah.

594
00:33:22,871 --> 00:33:28,470
just because the services are specialized and they're trying to differentiate from each other or whatever, they keep pulling away from you all the time.

595
00:33:28,470 --> 00:33:32,149
So, it's hard to just like keep them close enough to continue to be meaningful and interesting.

596
00:33:32,149 --> 00:33:35,909
I run into that a lot in my work because I have a variety of instruments.

597
00:33:35,909 --> 00:33:39,750
and like say a digital multimeter and people choose specific multimeters.

598
00:33:39,750 --> 00:33:43,190
because they want this one specific piece of functionality.

599
00:33:43,190 --> 00:33:46,230
and then if you put that in the generic interface then you run into problems.

600
00:33:46,230 --> 00:33:49,110
and you're breaking all the solid rules or whatever and things like that.

601
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:51,750
Yeah. Yeah. It's that's where it gets really tricky is yeah.

602
00:33:51,750 --> 00:33:54,789
when there's differentiation like how do you deal with that?

603
00:33:54,789 --> 00:33:57,909
And of course these are competing companies so they're trying really hard to differentiate.

604
00:33:57,909 --> 00:33:59,509
So, it's like this never- ending battle basically.

605
00:33:59,519 --> 00:34:05,831
to try to figure out some way to make all of those pieces fit together as they continue. to try to pull apart.

606
00:34:05,840 --> 00:34:11,270
Let's change topics a little bit and talk about anchor and local.host.

607
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:15,270
Sure. Can you talk a little bit about what Ankor is.

608
00:34:15,270 --> 00:34:16,790
and what it does and what need you're trying to fill?

609
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:20,950
Relates to our discussion earlier of how OpenSSL is kind of a beast to work with.

610
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:29,270
you know, like increasingly people see the need and value to do encryption and to have certificates.

611
00:34:29,270 --> 00:34:33,510
but they're still kind of a pain to actually work on and work with.

612
00:34:33,510 --> 00:34:37,750
And so the goal with Ankor is really to make them more accessible and easy to use.

613
00:34:37,750 --> 00:34:42,310
so that everybody can encrypt whenever and wherever they need to.

614
00:34:42,310 --> 00:34:43,909
And so we're slowly but surely trying to chip away at that problem.

615
00:34:43,909 --> 00:34:48,950
and provide more, you know, capabilities to do that more readily.

616
00:34:48,950 --> 00:34:52,710
I think ease of use is really important when it comes to security stuff like this.

617
00:34:52,710 --> 00:34:56,389
I was listening to a somebody talk about security the other day.

618
00:34:56,389 --> 00:34:57,910
and she mentioned that a simple thing you can do is.

619
00:34:57,910 --> 00:34:59,750
when you Google like how to do something.

620
00:34:59,750 --> 00:35:03,349
to just add a how to do it securely onto the end.

621
00:35:03,349 --> 00:35:06,231
because everybody always puts like the easy answer like oh I can't access this file. What should I do?

622
00:35:06,231 --> 00:35:12,630
Oh change the permissions and make them wide open. or oh my SSL certificate's not working. Oh well here's how you disable the SSL certificate.

623
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:14,390
Yes.

624
00:35:14,390 --> 00:35:18,711
The number of times I've seen the response to security questions be here is how you turn off. HTTPS.

625
00:35:18,711 --> 00:35:21,109
I can't even begin to count.

626
00:35:21,109 --> 00:35:22,790
I mean, and it just kind of hurts me.

627
00:35:22,790 --> 00:35:25,990
Makes me die a little bit more inside every time I see that, you know.

628
00:35:25,990 --> 00:35:31,590
because it's not a good sustainable reasonable approach, you know, but it gets the problem solved quickly. So, it's tempting.

629
00:35:31,590 --> 00:35:34,790
So, yeah. Or like turn off the firewall or something similar.

630
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:39,670
Yep. For sure. Those are often those are good as a first step just to see like,

631
00:35:39,670 --> 00:35:41,430
okay, that's what's causing my problem.

632
00:35:41,430 --> 00:35:44,151
But if you stop there and say, "Oh, I solve my problem." You walk away.

633
00:35:44,151 --> 00:35:45,910
Well, you've just created more problems.

634
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:53,750
Yeah, for sure. So, how does your you have some command line tools and stuff to make things easier for developers.

635
00:35:53,750 --> 00:35:57,910
How does it help solve that problem of like, oh, I could just do this?

636
00:35:57,910 --> 00:36:01,510
Is there an equally easy solution with your command line tools to solve some of those problems?

637
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:08,069
Yeah, in particular with the Anchor CLI, we've developed what we call LCL.host.

638
00:36:08,079 --> 00:36:13,430
And so the idea with that is to try as much as possible to make it really easy.

639
00:36:13,440 --> 00:36:17,750
for you to get encryption working on your local machine.

640
00:36:17,750 --> 00:36:21,670
So you're you know you're running your app server in whichever language.

641
00:36:21,670 --> 00:36:27,589
and you want it to serve secure content to your browser on your machine.

642
00:36:27,589 --> 00:36:30,550
Generally this is uh challenging because a lot of the ways that you would get certificates normally.

643
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:35,430
like let's encrypt or things like that you need to do DNS management and other things.

644
00:36:35,430 --> 00:36:38,390
and like you know most people probably don't necessarily have DNS pointing to their local machine in a.

645
00:36:38,390 --> 00:36:40,790
way that they can easily do that.

646
00:36:40,790 --> 00:36:46,870
and so historically you would basically have to provision the dreaded self-signed searchs you know.

647
00:36:46,870 --> 00:36:50,790
which again you have to like use cryptic commands to actually generate them.

648
00:36:50,790 --> 00:36:54,069
but then can also be challenging because now you've solved that problem for you.

649
00:36:54,069 --> 00:36:57,190
but if you're on a team of developers which most people probably are.

650
00:36:57,190 --> 00:36:58,710
This is still a problem for everyone else on the team, right?

651
00:36:58,720 --> 00:37:00,470
And there's a lot of examples.

652
00:37:00,470 --> 00:37:05,270
For instance, if you're trying to use like Stripe. or Apple Pay or something and you're trying to develop on that.

653
00:37:05,270 --> 00:37:10,390
like you can't actually use those interfaces in your browser if it's not a secure context, right?

654
00:37:10,390 --> 00:37:13,670
So, it just doesn't work.

655
00:37:13,670 --> 00:37:15,589
So, the idea with a lot of the LCL host stuff is to make it as easy as.

656
00:37:15,599 --> 00:37:19,829
possible to do that. So we have a single command basically. that you can run that just goes through the steps.

657
00:37:19,829 --> 00:37:24,069
that are necessary to provision a certificate for your machine.

658
00:37:24,069 --> 00:37:25,589
to install it where it needs to be in your trust store.

659
00:37:25,589 --> 00:37:27,190
so that your browser will recognize and use it.

660
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:32,310
and other tools will recognize and use it. and then you know within just a few minutes you're up and running.

661
00:37:32,310 --> 00:37:36,790
and you can see again whatever your web app is delivered through a secure context.

662
00:37:36,790 --> 00:37:39,030
so that you can get back to doing whatever you need to do.

663
00:37:39,040 --> 00:37:43,829
So previously we talked about web APIs a lot.

664
00:37:43,829 --> 00:37:47,430
Do you see a major difference between web APIs and CLI APIs?

665
00:37:47,440 --> 00:37:49,510
Sometimes.

666
00:37:49,510 --> 00:37:51,510
I mean, it depends a lot on just like what the use cases are.

667
00:37:51,510 --> 00:37:55,990
So, you know, there are parts of our API that we've developed basically because.

668
00:37:56,000 --> 00:38:02,310
you know, like the LCL command that we were just talking about is trying to accomplish certain things.

669
00:38:02,310 --> 00:38:06,069
I'm not sure if end users normally would be trying to do those things.

670
00:38:06,069 --> 00:38:09,030
or to be doing them in quite the same way.

671
00:38:09,030 --> 00:38:14,311
So I think it might make sense to for instance provide the normal CRUD operations across your different. resources.

672
00:38:14,311 --> 00:38:16,790
because you know eventually everybody's going to need them or whatever.

673
00:38:16,790 --> 00:38:24,630
but maybe to have particular actions or additional endpoints that are specifically for the CLI. because again it just needs to do things sometimes.

674
00:38:24,630 --> 00:38:26,310
that other people don't necessarily need to do or care to do.

675
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:28,550
And so I don't know it's just a mix.

676
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:31,750
It's just whatever is needed for one or the other.

677
00:38:31,750 --> 00:38:33,430
I think the other thing is like we have for better.

678
00:38:33,430 --> 00:38:40,630
or worse designed certain CLI actions that actually require multiple API calls bene be behind the scenes.

679
00:38:40,630 --> 00:38:43,750
and that can get hairy because now it's not impossible.

680
00:38:43,760 --> 00:38:47,910
but it's very difficult to have transactional boundaries around all those actions right.

681
00:38:47,910 --> 00:38:50,150
so it's very easy then to end up in situations.

682
00:38:50,150 --> 00:38:53,270
where things end up in weird partial states right.

683
00:38:53,270 --> 00:38:54,790
because two of the changes succeeded and then the third one failed.

684
00:38:54,800 --> 00:38:59,190
and so now do you roll that all back or something.

685
00:38:59,190 --> 00:39:02,230
whereas if you just created a single API endpoint point.

686
00:39:02,230 --> 00:39:05,829
that was basically directly tied to that particular CLI action.

687
00:39:05,829 --> 00:39:09,190
it would be much easier to wrap all of the things in transaction boundary. Does that make sense?

688
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:11,030
Yeah.

689
00:39:11,030 --> 00:39:12,550
If I'm understanding correctly, in this particular case,

690
00:39:12,550 --> 00:39:16,551
your CLI is actually calling an API behind the scenes, like a web API. Okay.

691
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:18,551
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

692
00:39:18,551 --> 00:39:20,471
So, we definitely have cases where. Yeah.

693
00:39:20,471 --> 00:39:24,390
the CLI itself is calling a web API and doing multiple different things.

694
00:39:24,390 --> 00:39:26,069
And usually from the way it's set up, that's just fine.

695
00:39:26,069 --> 00:39:30,870
But yeah, there are some edge cases where we've ended up in outliers where.

696
00:39:30,870 --> 00:39:33,670
yeah, there's stuff that's in weird state because only some of the commands succeeded.

697
00:39:33,670 --> 00:39:39,190
because we don't have a specific API on the website. that is wrapping all of that in a transaction.

698
00:39:39,200 --> 00:39:41,589
So they don't match one to one necessarily.

699
00:39:41,599 --> 00:39:43,191
Yeah. Yeah.

700
00:39:43,191 --> 00:39:47,350
There's a lot of cases where the CLI is actually doing a few things for you. instead of just one big thing.

701
00:39:47,359 --> 00:39:52,470
So is that the case of then giving them the easy solution.

702
00:39:52,470 --> 00:39:54,310
that is run this one CLI command.

703
00:39:54,310 --> 00:39:56,630
versus making several different API calls for the more advanced user?

704
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:58,310
Yeah, that is definitely the case.

705
00:39:58,310 --> 00:40:01,510
And I mean we've already seen that in some cases.

706
00:40:01,510 --> 00:40:04,790
too where it's sort of like we are doing a somewhat more complicated thing in the CLI.

707
00:40:04,790 --> 00:40:06,550
to make it easier for the users.

708
00:40:06,550 --> 00:40:09,109
But now we're starting to identify that oh actually this is just something.

709
00:40:09,109 --> 00:40:11,510
that users are going to want to do more generally.

710
00:40:11,510 --> 00:40:15,829
And so now we're moving towards developing some new API endpoints.

711
00:40:15,829 --> 00:40:19,190
that will do the more complicated thing that the CLI used to do.

712
00:40:19,190 --> 00:40:21,190
and then move the CLI to use that endpoint instead of again.

713
00:40:21,190 --> 00:40:28,069
like having it do four operations and take all the return values from all of. that and knit them together in some particular way. and then give you the output from that.

714
00:40:28,069 --> 00:40:31,109
You know, it's like maybe that should just be one API call that does all of that.

715
00:40:31,119 --> 00:40:35,910
So, you know, we continue to learn, we continue to make mistakes, we continue to tweak and iterate.

716
00:40:35,910 --> 00:40:37,910
Again, you know, trying to be agile like we talked about.

717
00:40:37,920 --> 00:40:39,430
that kind of gels with this idea.

718
00:40:39,430 --> 00:40:43,750
that I've heard a lot lately of pushing the complexity down and making things easier.

719
00:40:43,760 --> 00:40:49,671
in the sense that you know the typical use case is to do these three things all in. order.

720
00:40:49,671 --> 00:40:51,750
and it's it's like the 90% use case make a method.

721
00:40:51,750 --> 00:40:53,430
or make an action that does just that.

722
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:55,190
Yeah.

723
00:40:55,190 --> 00:40:57,430
And I don't know that I have gotten all the way into doing.

724
00:40:57,430 --> 00:40:59,589
that in the web API as much as I might like.

725
00:40:59,589 --> 00:41:02,870
but we've definitely done that a lot in the CLI, I would say.

726
00:41:02,870 --> 00:41:04,550
where for instance the LCL command that we mentioned,

727
00:41:04,550 --> 00:41:10,069
like behind the scenes, it actually runs, I think it's either four or five CLI commands.

728
00:41:10,069 --> 00:41:12,390
And you can technically run each of those in order yourself,

729
00:41:12,390 --> 00:41:15,830
but like in many cases, why would you, right? Like let the tool do this for me.

730
00:41:15,839 --> 00:41:19,510
And you know just generally I think I think more.

731
00:41:19,510 --> 00:41:21,670
and more about this notion of things like CRUD things like actions.

732
00:41:21,670 --> 00:41:26,630
but also having other action other possibilities that are more like a workflow almost right.

733
00:41:26,630 --> 00:41:29,910
like so that's the sort of notion of like there's actually these three actions.

734
00:41:29,910 --> 00:41:33,030
that people very commonly want to do together. so maybe we should have a sort of workflow.

735
00:41:33,030 --> 00:41:38,069
that encompasses those set of things and just can run end to end.

736
00:41:38,069 --> 00:41:41,990
and maybe it includes some hints or extra information that you wouldn't get if you ran them individually.

737
00:41:41,990 --> 00:41:43,589
because this is a broader thing or maybe it doesn't I don't know.

738
00:41:43,599 --> 00:41:47,829
but it gives you some context to provide something specifically to suit.

739
00:41:47,829 --> 00:41:50,870
that use case in a way that is more tailored.

740
00:41:50,870 --> 00:41:53,750
that provides a nicer experience that gives the person just what they need.

741
00:41:53,760 --> 00:41:58,069
especially again when it's a super common use case that happens day in day out.

742
00:41:58,079 --> 00:42:00,711
Yeah. So maybe they do step one and.

743
00:42:00,711 --> 00:42:04,790
then it prompts do you want to continue to step two. or you can just cancel out or something like that.

744
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:12,309
Yeah. Yeah. So wrapping things up talk that I heard you give recently you mentioned an HTTP API design guide.

745
00:42:12,319 --> 00:42:14,309
Mhm.

746
00:42:14,309 --> 00:42:15,910
Where can people get that and what is it and what does it cover?

747
00:42:15,920 --> 00:42:19,430
Sure.

748
00:42:19,430 --> 00:42:26,630
It's on the inter agent in ter agent GitHub and it's was from when I worked at Heroku.

749
00:42:26,630 --> 00:42:30,309
So at Heroku I was the lead designer for the version 3 API.

750
00:42:30,309 --> 00:42:35,910
and so coming out of that we tried to basically kind of like create like a style guide.

751
00:42:35,910 --> 00:42:39,670
I don't know if you're familiar with the notion of style guides from say like writing or whatever.

752
00:42:39,680 --> 00:42:44,069
There's the is it the Chicago style guide. and there's like the AP style guide and things like that.

753
00:42:44,069 --> 00:42:46,230
that kind of say like here's when you should.

754
00:42:46,230 --> 00:42:50,390
or shouldn't use commas or apostrophes or dashes or you know things like that.

755
00:42:50,390 --> 00:42:54,790
So the idea was kind of trying to copy from something like.

756
00:42:54,790 --> 00:42:58,471
that and at least in part it was like geared towards us doing that internally. I've designed this API.

757
00:42:58,471 --> 00:42:59,990
I I now want to try to explain to you how.

758
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:04,150
and why I've done certain things and how you should do them.

759
00:43:04,150 --> 00:43:07,190
so that it'll be easier for other people to contribute and participate.

760
00:43:07,190 --> 00:43:12,230
and for us to continue to have something that's consistent to have less surprises,

761
00:43:12,230 --> 00:43:13,829
better principle of least surprise, things like that.

762
00:43:13,829 --> 00:43:19,270
And having done that, we decided that a lot of the advice was pretty generically useful and interesting.

763
00:43:19,270 --> 00:43:21,030
and so it would be good to share that more broadly.

764
00:43:21,030 --> 00:43:24,230
And so we opened it up and released it to the public.

765
00:43:24,230 --> 00:43:27,590
And yeah, it tries to cover just a lot of things like talks about the actions pattern. that I mentioned.

766
00:43:27,590 --> 00:43:31,670
It talks about, you know, versioning and ideas and recommendations for that.

767
00:43:31,670 --> 00:43:35,589
and just kind of tries to cover a lot of different things of how to try to again.

768
00:43:35,599 --> 00:43:37,829
have an API that's consistent.

769
00:43:37,829 --> 00:43:42,950
that people can kind of understand and navigate that doesn't have too much surprise ideally.

770
00:43:42,950 --> 00:43:46,309
Yeah, just first principles for a lot of that stuff to hopefully help make it easier.

771
00:43:46,309 --> 00:43:48,870
for people to I guess learn from my taste.

772
00:43:48,870 --> 00:43:50,470
It's a cookbook almost or something, right?

773
00:43:50,470 --> 00:43:53,510
of sort of like I have developed this taste for APIs or whatever.

774
00:43:53,510 --> 00:43:55,510
and I'm trying to explain to someone how.

775
00:43:55,510 --> 00:43:57,829
and why you might want to follow along with those examples.

776
00:43:57,839 --> 00:44:03,270
Do you have any idea of the adoption of this guide? Like do you know anyone who's been using it?

777
00:44:03,280 --> 00:44:07,270
I mean got a bunch of stars I guess on GitHub.

778
00:44:07,270 --> 00:44:10,230
That's like a good sign probably.

779
00:44:10,230 --> 00:44:13,510
I mean I've been told by people that they found it very interesting and influential at the time.

780
00:44:13,520 --> 00:44:21,270
because not a lot of other people had tried to talk openly about that kind of stuff, right? like you could go and see what somebody's API was,

781
00:44:21,270 --> 00:44:22,950
but you didn't really see an explanation of how or why.

782
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:25,430
And so I gather it was influential,

783
00:44:25,430 --> 00:44:30,790
but yeah, like just through anecdotes really and I guess through the star counts. So I hope it helps people.

784
00:44:30,790 --> 00:44:32,790
I mean that's why we do stuff like that.

785
00:44:32,790 --> 00:44:35,030
So great.

786
00:44:35,030 --> 00:44:37,670
Is there anything else about APIs that we haven't talked about that you would like to mention?

787
00:44:37,680 --> 00:44:41,910
I mean another relating to open API and things like that too.

788
00:44:41,910 --> 00:44:43,990
The other part of that that I found to be quite useful.

789
00:44:44,000 --> 00:44:46,150
that we didn't talk about is mocking.

790
00:44:46,160 --> 00:44:49,829
which is if you have an open API spec, there are a lot of nice tools actually.

791
00:44:49,839 --> 00:44:56,230
that will basically start a server that will provide all of the API endpoints that you have defined.

792
00:44:56,230 --> 00:45:03,030
that you can actually start to write a client against and get back not real but realistic responses.

793
00:45:03,030 --> 00:45:05,589
I found that to be super helpful.

794
00:45:05,589 --> 00:45:09,349
Again, going to the notion of trying to make the mistakes in the cheapest way possible.

795
00:45:09,349 --> 00:45:11,190
So a lot of my flow now is sort of like write a spec.

796
00:45:11,200 --> 00:45:13,430
that is what I think I need.

797
00:45:13,430 --> 00:45:15,430
then start to write a client against it.

798
00:45:15,430 --> 00:45:20,630
and that usually helps me to reveal quite quickly what I forgot what I overlooked.

799
00:45:20,630 --> 00:45:25,109
and then adjusting the spec and then actually go into the real implementation of it once.

800
00:45:25,119 --> 00:45:28,470
that spec is settled down. It isn't a way that I had developed historically.

801
00:45:28,480 --> 00:45:31,750
and having stumbled upon that I found it really useful.

802
00:45:31,750 --> 00:45:33,589
So I think that's a good one to keep in mind for people.

803
00:45:33,589 --> 00:45:35,990
So in that case then you write the client.

804
00:45:35,990 --> 00:45:38,551
or a sample client before you actually write the server. Is that.

805
00:45:38,560 --> 00:45:40,790
Yes.

806
00:45:40,790 --> 00:45:45,750
Um for sure because again like for me. yeah I guess I don't want to end up in the situation. where I like do all of the work of writing the server code.

807
00:45:45,750 --> 00:45:51,430
and then realize that I had like fundamental misunderstandings about what I was trying to do.

808
00:45:51,430 --> 00:45:53,270
And so doing the client will often help me to get there faster.

809
00:45:53,280 --> 00:45:55,829
Right?

810
00:45:55,829 --> 00:45:58,950
So whether that's doing some in my case again I was working on CLI stuff a.

811
00:45:58,950 --> 00:46:01,829
I would actually like start to write out the CLI operation.

812
00:46:01,829 --> 00:46:06,150
and make it work at least in the right shape of things working.

813
00:46:06,150 --> 00:46:07,670
before I'd ever implemented the server.

814
00:46:07,670 --> 00:46:09,589
and that would quickly reveal to me,

815
00:46:09,589 --> 00:46:11,349
oh, I left out two or three fields that really ought to be here.

816
00:46:11,349 --> 00:46:14,710
or, you know, this doesn't get me this piece of information that I needed.

817
00:46:14,710 --> 00:46:16,950
So, do I add this to this API endpoint?

818
00:46:16,950 --> 00:46:21,750
Do I like make another call to another API endpoint? Do I make a new API endpoint that includes both of them?

819
00:46:21,750 --> 00:46:25,430
Like, you know, what do I need to do to get to where I need to be?

820
00:46:25,430 --> 00:46:30,150
definitely has helped me to again be more agile basically.

821
00:46:30,150 --> 00:46:31,670
because I can iterate before doing the server implementation as well.

822
00:46:31,680 --> 00:46:35,190
like because at the end of the day you're going to have to implement the server.

823
00:46:35,190 --> 00:46:38,630
and the client and probably the spec. and so yeah that just feels like the faster way.

824
00:46:38,630 --> 00:46:43,109
Usually the client is simpler in my experience than the server is.

825
00:46:43,109 --> 00:46:46,390
So work on the spec first which is it's the simplest and cheapest then the client second.

826
00:46:46,390 --> 00:46:48,309
because it's the next simplest and cheapest I guess.

827
00:46:48,309 --> 00:46:52,550
and then defer the server till the last because you have the most information then.

828
00:46:52,550 --> 00:46:57,671
so you least likely to make mistakes you know more likely to be able to just push through. and get it done.

829
00:46:57,680 --> 00:47:00,470
That sounds like great advice. Thank you.

